{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:02:16Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 145, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t// Every network call goes through here: components never call fetch directly,\n2\t// so the base URL and error handling stay in one place.\n3\tconst BASE = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE || 'http://localhost:8000'\n4\t\n5\tasync function request(path, options = {}) {\n6\t  const res = await fetch(BASE + path, {\n7\t    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n8\t    ...options,\n9\t  })\n10\t  if (!res.ok) {\n11\t    const detail = await res.json().catch(() => null)\n12\t    throw new Error(detail?.detail || `request failed (${res.status})`)\n13\t  }\n14\t  return res.status === 204 ? null : res.json()\n15\t}\n16\t\n17\texport const listTasks = () => request('/api/tasks')\n18\texport const createTask = (title, priority) =>\n19\t  request('/api/tasks', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ title, priority }) })\n20\texport const completeTask = (id) => request(`/api/tasks/${id}/done`, { method: 'POST' })\n21\texport const updateTask = (id, title) =>\n22\t  request(`/api/tasks/${id}`, { method: 'PATCH', body: JSON.stringify({ title }) })\n23\texport const setPriority = (id, priority) =>\n24\t  request(`/api/tasks/${id}/priority`, { method: 'PATCH', body: JSON.stringify({ priority }) })\n25\texport const deleteTask = (id) => request(`/api/tasks/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' })\n26\texport const clearCompleted = () => request('/api/tasks/completed', { method: 'DELETE' })\n27\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_018eRWwzitQJD7osk2DqJ3hy", "session_id": "a08aef13-3276-4679-921c-60d5cb97c247"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:02:24Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 146, "kind": "message", "text": "The frontend `clearCompleted()` at `frontend/src/api.js:26` matches the new `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` route and expects a JSON body (`{count: int}`), which the new `ClearResult` response provides correctly.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe backend half of Taiga #9 was genuinely missing (confirmed by the review), and an earlier partial attempt had left some inconsistent/duplicated state across files, which I found and cleaned up during implementation. Final state:\n\n- **`backend/app/models.py`** \u2014 added `ClearResult(BaseModel)` with a single `count: int` field.\n- **`backend/app/store.py:94-100`** \u2014 added `TaskStore.clear_completed() -> int`, matching the shape of `delete()`: removes all done tasks, calls `self._save()` once only if anything changed, returns the count removed.\n- **`backend/app/main.py:56-58`** \u2014 added `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` \u2192 `ClearResult`, declared **above** `@app.delete(\"/api/tasks/{task_id}\")` at what's now line 61, avoiding the route-ordering hazard the spec flagged.\n- **`backend/tests/test_tasks.py`** \u2014 4 new tests: empty store (AC6), no-completed-tasks no-op (AC1), mixed removal with open tasks preserved (AC2), no `done: true` tasks remain afterward (AC3).\n- **`backend/tests/test_persistence.py:87-101`** \u2014 `test_cleared_completed_tasks_do_not_reappear_after_restart` already existed from the earlier attempt and correctly covers AC5; left as-is.\n\nAC4 (filter-independence) has no backend dimension \u2014 the store has no concept of a filter, so `clear_completed()` is inherently store-wide.\n\nVerified: full backend suite passes (35/35, `python3 -m pytest -q`), including the 4 new tests run in isolation. Frontend `clearCompleted()` in `api.js:26` already matches this contract exactly, so no frontend changes were needed.", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "a08aef13-3276-4679-921c-60d5cb97c247"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:02:24Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 147, "kind": "status", "text": "The frontend `clearCompleted()` at `frontend/src/api.js:26` matches the new `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` route and expects a JSON body (`{count: int}`), which the new `ClearResult` response provides correctly.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe backend half of Taiga #9 was genuinely missing (confirmed by the review), and an earlier partial attempt had left some inconsistent/duplicated state across files, which I found and cleaned up during implementation. Final state:\n\n- **`backend/app/models.py`** \u2014 added `ClearResult(BaseModel)` with a single `count: int` field.\n- **`backend/app/store.py:94-100`** \u2014 added `TaskStore.clear_completed() -> int`, matching the shape of `delete()`: removes all done tasks, calls `self._save()` once only if anything changed, returns the count removed.\n- **`backend/app/main.py:56-58`** \u2014 added `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` \u2192 `ClearResult`, declared **above** `@app.delete(\"/api/tasks/{task_id}\")` at what's now line 61, avoiding the route-ordering hazard the spec flagged.\n- **`backend/tests/test_tasks.py`** \u2014 4 new tests: empty store (AC6), no-completed-tasks no-op (AC1), mixed removal with open tasks preserved (AC2), no `done: true` tasks remain afterward (AC3).\n- **`backend/tests/test_persistence.py:87-101`** \u2014 `test_cleared_completed_tasks_do_not_reappear_after_restart` already existed from the earlier attempt and correctly covers AC5; left as-is.\n\nAC4 (filter-independence) has no backend dimension \u2014 the store has no concept of a filter, so `clear_completed()` is inherently store-wide.\n\nVerified: full backend suite passes (35/35, `python3 -m pytest -q`), including the 4 new tests run in isolation. Frontend `clearCompleted()` in `api.js:26` already matches this contract exactly, so no frontend changes were needed.", "meta": {"native_type": "result", "subtype": "success", "session_id": "a08aef13-3276-4679-921c-60d5cb97c247", "is_error": false, "total_cost_usd": 0.9632201999999999}}
